WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.441 --> 00:00:11.444
Hi. My name is Margaret Gardel and I run a biophysics lab at the

00:00:11.445 --> 00:00:14.680
University of Chicago. Today I am going to tell you about my favorite

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:17.116
problem at the interface of biology and physics.

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:21.220
When you first learn about cells, you think about the structure and

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:25.424
function of individual organelles. What you learn much less about is

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:29.428
the stuff that takes up the rest of the space within the cell known as the

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:34.567
cytoplasm. They cytoplasm is about 50% water. The rest of the

00:00:34.568 --> 00:00:39.705
volume is filled with densely packed globular protein, small organelles,

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:44.277
and dense filamentous networks known as the actin, microtubules and

00:00:44.278 --> 00:00:49.382
intermediate filament cytoskeletons. So this complicated material

00:00:49.383 --> 00:00:53.319
can be thought of as a complex fluid or visco-elastic solid.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.923
The fact that you could call it either liquid or a solid depends.

00:00:56.924 --> 00:01:00.960
It is said that it's mechanics on both the length and time scales you are

00:01:00.961 --> 00:01:06.799
considering. This field of soft condensed matter physics is well equipped

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.070
to build physical models of such complicated materials.

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:15.441
The fact that the cytoplasm is made up of macromolecules

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:18.177
means that thermal effects are going to be important.

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:25.017
If we now go down to the scale of individual proteins, we can, in this

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:29.522
animation, can start to see the effects of thermal energy at this

00:01:29.523 --> 00:01:33.159
nanometer length scale. You see these randomized motions of

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.561
individual macromolecules known as Brownian motion

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:40.933
drives random agitation and collisions between macromolecules.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:44.203
You also get a sense of how crowed the cytoplasm is.

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:49.475
All soft material have this type of thermal effects at play

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:54.347
and so we have really built physical models of such soft

00:01:54.348 --> 00:01:57.750
materials in the physical world.

00:01:57.751 --> 00:02:02.188
What makes the cytoplasm unique is it also contains an abundance

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:06.626
of mechanoenzymes. That is proteins that can convert chemical energy

00:02:06.627 --> 00:02:10.930
to do mechanical work. An excellent example of this is molecular motors.

00:02:10.931 --> 00:02:15.134
Here is a movie and animation of a motor protein, kinesin, walking

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:20.339
along a microtubule track. Right, the kinesin is taking in a chemical

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.977
energy source and is using that to do mechanical work, to drive its

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:29.415
motility and generate local stresses. The fact that the cytoplasm

00:02:29.416 --> 00:02:31.717
contains all of these mechanoenzymes means that there is an

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:37.523
external energy source in addition to just thermal effects. This drives

00:02:37.524 --> 00:02:39.725
the cytoplasm out of thermodynamic equilibrium

00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:43.596
and makes it a very unique material.

00:02:43.597 --> 00:02:47.433
So the cytoplasm is this soft material that is driven far from

00:02:47.434 --> 00:02:50.136
thermodynamic equilibrium. It has no analogs of

00:02:50.137 --> 00:02:54.106
similar types of material in the physical world. This means that new

00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:57.210
physics is required understand the physics of the cytoplasm.

00:02:57.211 --> 00:03:02.515
I'm going to talk you briefly about two examples

00:03:02.516 --> 00:03:04.984
of where physics can play an important role in understanding

00:03:04.985 --> 00:03:09.088
aspects of cell physiology. The first is intercellular transport.

00:03:09.089 --> 00:03:13.559
For many different aspects of life process in a cell, they have to

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.630
move organelles and molecules from one side of the cell to the other.

00:03:17.631 --> 00:03:22.101
The types of cargo that need to be transported range from

00:03:22.102 --> 00:03:26.672
small molecules like water to up to micron size organelles.

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:30.743
Now if we just think about thermal forces and diffusion

00:03:30.744 --> 00:03:34.514
it's incapable of moving even the smallest cargo over very

00:03:34.515 --> 00:03:36.649
large distances in a reasonable time.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:40.920
Therefore you need motor proteins transport this cargo

00:03:40.921 --> 00:03:44.457
along the filamentous tracks. Now if the cytoplasm

00:03:44.458 --> 00:03:48.394
were just simply water, the effective drag felt on any

00:03:48.395 --> 00:03:51.564
of these cargo would be negligible and motor proteins

00:03:51.565 --> 00:03:54.800
could do this quite easily. However the cytoplasm is this

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:58.204
very complex environment and these cargo are going

00:03:58.205 --> 00:04:02.775
to feel very large resistances. How do motor protein overcome

00:04:02.776 --> 00:04:05.745
this problem to really effectively move cargo?

00:04:05.746 --> 00:04:09.749
So if we just think about cargo moving in the cytoplasm,

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.019
depending on your size, you are going to feel a different local

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.489
environment. Small molecules are going to be able to diffuse

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:20.660
rapidly and go in between spaces of globular proteins

00:04:20.661 --> 00:04:24.030
whereas large cargo are going to be effectively trapped.

00:04:24.031 --> 00:04:27.900
This means that the type of material you are considering

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:31.304
in the cytoplasm really depends on the landscape you are looking at.

00:04:31.305 --> 00:04:35.675
It will mostly be liquid like at small length scales and it will appear

00:04:35.676 --> 00:04:39.679
like a solid at long length scales. This transition between a liquid and

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:45.117
solid occurs at length scales relevant to organelles. Most proteins

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:48.154
will experience a liquid like environment whereas chromosomes

00:04:48.155 --> 00:04:51.157
will experience a more solid like environment.

00:04:51.158 --> 00:04:58.297
Now how does a cell stay well mixed to be able to transport all of its objects and

00:04:58.298 --> 00:04:59.932
and get them in the right place in the right time?

00:04:59.933 --> 00:05:06.172
Well one recent idea is that the enzymatic activity within the cytoplasm

00:05:06.173 --> 00:05:11.611
actually serves to mix the cytoplasm up. This is akin to

00:05:11.612 --> 00:05:17.416
the convective mixing you see a pot of water on a stove top.

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:20.453
Right, that eviction really mixes the water in a way that much

00:05:20.454 --> 00:05:25.725
better than diffusion. So this act of stirring is then thought to liquidize

00:05:25.726 --> 00:05:29.629
the cytoplasm over all the relevant length scales

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:32.131
so the chromosomes also feel a liquid like environment.

00:05:32.132 --> 00:05:37.870
Now the question is does the enzymatic activity really stir the cytoplasm

00:05:37.871 --> 00:05:41.707
and is it possible that this enzymatic activity could be regulated

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:46.345
to regulate the amount of stirring during different times of the

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:50.183
cell metabolism. That is could a cell effectively transition

00:05:50.184 --> 00:05:53.753
itself from being liquid like to solid like depending on its life stage?

00:05:53.754 --> 00:05:58.391
The second example I would like to talk about is in cell morphogenesis.

00:05:58.392 --> 00:06:02.428
At the outer cell membrane, there is a dense network of

00:06:02.429 --> 00:06:08.467
filamentous proteins known as the actin cortex and this actin cortex

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:12.471
is responsible for cell shape changes you see during cell division,

00:06:12.472 --> 00:06:15.107
cell migration and tissue morphogenesis.

00:06:15.108 --> 00:06:19.745
So what is the mechanics of this outer polymer network

00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:22.815
that enables a cell to do these robust shape changes?

00:06:22.816 --> 00:06:26.385
So to get an idea of what this actin cortex looks like

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:31.123
you can see this fluorescence microscopy image of a reconstituted actin cortex

00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:35.728
where actin filaments are labeled in red. You can see it is quite dense.

00:06:35.729 --> 00:06:41.200
Over a 100 micromolar. And it is a very thin, dense sheet of actin at

00:06:41.201 --> 00:06:44.704
at the plasma membrane. And so what are the mechanics of

00:06:44.705 --> 00:06:46.205
this thin sheet?

00:06:46.206 --> 00:06:49.809
Well actin networks are semi-flexible polymers.

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:53.813
And what semi-flexible means is that their banding rigidity is comparable to

00:06:53.814 --> 00:06:59.285
thermal energy and the ratio between these two components

00:06:59.286 --> 00:07:02.588
gives you a length scale that tells you the persistence length,

00:07:02.589 --> 00:07:05.791
the length scales for which the polymer maintains its rigidity

00:07:05.792 --> 00:07:09.862
and since this length scale is 17 microns, that means a actin filament

00:07:09.863 --> 00:07:14.734
will sometimes behave flexible over larger length scales and

00:07:14.735 --> 00:07:17.637
behave more rigid over shorter length scales.

00:07:17.638 --> 00:07:22.608
Semi-flexible polymers are almost exclusively found in biological polymers

00:07:22.609 --> 00:07:26.279
and confer the networks they form with very unique behaviors not seen

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:30.183
in physical polymer networks and this area of trying to understand how this

00:07:30.184 --> 00:07:33.853
semi-flexible affects force transmission is an active area of research.

00:07:33.854 --> 00:07:39.091
Secondly, we know that actin filaments are not static but they are actively assembling

00:07:39.092 --> 00:07:43.763
and disassembling and so how does this active assembly and disassembly of

00:07:43.764 --> 00:07:47.433
the polymers affect their mechanical response to the networks?

00:07:47.434 --> 00:07:52.038
We simply just don't have the models that integrate polymerization dynamics

00:07:52.039 --> 00:07:56.275
with the mechanics. Some speculation is the polymerization dynamics are

00:07:56.276 --> 00:08:01.514
essential for fluidizing the cortex and enabling large shape changes while

00:08:01.515 --> 00:08:05.318
maintaining a uniform density during processes like cell division.

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:10.356
So in addition to the actin filaments in the cortex, the main force generators

00:08:10.357 --> 00:08:14.660
in the cortex are myosin 2 motors. In this fluorescence image, myosin 2

00:08:14.661 --> 00:08:19.232
is seen as an elongated rod and at the molecular scale, these

00:08:19.233 --> 00:08:23.102
elongated rods are actually bipolar filaments of myosin 2

00:08:23.103 --> 00:08:28.574
motors that drive antiparallel sliding of actin filaments. So these myosin 2 motors

00:08:28.575 --> 00:08:34.213
generate local stresses. In contrast to the highly organized circameric structure

00:08:34.214 --> 00:08:40.653
actomyosin you seen in striated myofibrils in muscle cells, in non muscle cells

00:08:40.654 --> 00:08:46.559
you can see actomyosin is very disorganized. This means the myosin 2 is going to

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:52.532
generate forces that vary in space and time. So we can understand. We can start to

00:08:52.533 --> 00:08:57.136
explore how forces get transmitted in this very disorganized network

00:08:57.137 --> 00:09:00.373
by reconstituting again these are actin filaments with myosin 2 motors on

00:09:00.374 --> 00:09:05.478
top of it and we can start to see these random motions of myosin 2.

00:09:05.479 --> 00:09:09.815
So because the actin cytoskeleton has so many

00:09:09.816 --> 00:09:14.854
can be controlled spatially, we think that the architecture of the actin cytoskeleton is

00:09:14.855 --> 00:09:18.491
regulating the activity of the myosin 2 motors but we simply lack the

00:09:18.492 --> 00:09:22.428
models to understand what is driving these types of responses.

00:09:22.429 --> 00:09:27.667
And likewise, you can see that the myosin 2 is forming these dense aggregates.

00:09:27.668 --> 00:09:32.605
This type of aggregation is not seen in wild type cells and so what in the cytoplasm is

00:09:32.606 --> 00:09:38.845
is allowing myosin 2 contractile forces to not concentrate the cytoplasm

00:09:38.846 --> 00:09:41.047
in certain regions of the cell.

00:09:41.048 --> 00:09:47.520
So I’ve given you two examples of outstanding questions in cell mechanics

00:09:47.521 --> 00:09:52.091
but I think there is even a greater challenge to be had at this interface.

00:09:52.092 --> 00:09:57.230
For instance, we currently now just build models that are specific to

00:09:57.231 --> 00:10:01.434
individual aspects of cell physiology. That is we try to understand shape changes during

00:10:01.435 --> 00:10:06.572
cell division or cell morphogenesis or cell migration but could we build a universal

00:10:06.573 --> 00:10:12.044
model of cell mechanics that guides a cell in all its different shape changes

00:10:12.045 --> 00:10:17.216
through its entire life span? Can we really understand all the versatility

00:10:17.217 --> 00:10:24.223
in the cytoplasm that a cell experiences. Secondly, we have increasing knowledge of

00:10:24.224 --> 00:10:28.127
the molecular details of cellular processes however these molecular details

00:10:28.128 --> 00:10:33.633
do not confer the physical control parameters that are guiding the physics of

00:10:33.634 --> 00:10:38.070
the cytoplasm. So we don't have these control parameters and

00:10:38.071 --> 00:10:44.744
currently different models confer different amounts of importance to

00:10:44.745 --> 00:10:49.248
different parameters and so these need to be rectified and we need to have

00:10:49.249 --> 00:10:52.485
predictive theories that can then be experimentally tested

00:10:52.486 --> 00:10:54.487
to see which one of these are correct.

00:10:54.488 --> 00:10:58.324
Hopefully once we know the right control parameters,

00:10:58.325 --> 00:11:02.195
it will give us increased understanding of how the cytoplasm confers

00:11:02.196 --> 00:11:08.734
very robust responses in cells. That is, there ability to respond to a large range

00:11:08.735 --> 00:11:13.005
environmental stimuli and undergo very dramatic shape changes.

00:11:13.006 --> 00:11:17.910
It may even tell us how these very complicated cytoplasmic structures

00:11:17.911 --> 00:11:25.551
evolved to give us such a beautiful mechanical responses.

00:11:25.552 --> 00:11:29.288
And so these and many other questions at the interface of

00:11:29.289 --> 00:11:59.253
cell biology and physics will give us lots of challenges for years to come.

